      heading: "What a good interpretation looks like",
      body: "Examiners do not want a list of numbers. They want to know that you understand what the numbers say about a real business. A complete answer has four parts. First, state the change: gross margin fell from 38% to 33%. Second, say what it means: the business kept 5 cents less of every dollar of sales after paying for the goods. Third, give a likely cause: selling prices were cut, or suppliers charged more and the increase was not passed on. Fourth, suggest an action and its drawback: raise prices, but customers may go elsewhere.\n\nAlways use the figures in the question. If revenue rose while gross margin fell, say so, because that points to a price cut used to win sales. If expenses are given, work out whether they grew faster than revenue. A cause that fits the data earns the mark; a cause copied from a list may not. Avoid vague words such as bad management. Name the thing that changed.",
