  mcq: [
    { id: "mcq-6.2-1", question: "A business increased its revenue but its gross margin fell. Which is the most likely cause?", choices: ["Selling prices were reduced to attract more customers", "Rent increased during the year", "The owner reduced drawings", "Bank interest charges rose"], explanation: "Gross margin depends only on selling prices and the cost of goods sold. Lower prices increase sales volume while reducing the margin on each sale. Rent and interest are expenses that affect profit margin, and drawings affect neither." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-2", question: "Gross margin stayed at 40% but profit margin fell from 12% to 8%. What does this show?", choices: ["Expenses rose faster than revenue", "Cost of sales rose faster than revenue", "Selling prices were cut", "Closing inventory was overvalued"], explanation: "A steady gross margin rules out changes in prices, cost of sales or inventory valuation. The fall must come after gross profit, which means expenses took a bigger share of revenue." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-3", question: "Which action would improve the liquid (acid test) ratio?", choices: ["Selling surplus inventory for cash at cost", "Buying more inventory on credit", "Paying trade payables when the liquid ratio is below 1:1", "The owner taking extra drawings in cash"], explanation: "Selling inventory for cash turns a non-liquid asset into a liquid one, raising the ratio. Buying on credit raises liabilities without adding liquid assets. Paying payables when the ratio is below 1:1 reduces top and bottom by the same amount and lowers the ratio further. Drawings remove cash." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-4", question: "The rate of inventory turnover fell from 12 times to 8 times. What does this mean?", choices: ["Goods are held for longer before being sold", "Goods are sold more quickly", "Gross margin has increased", "Trade payables are being paid faster"], explanation: "Fewer times means the average inventory takes longer to sell: about 46 days instead of 30. It says nothing directly about margin or payables." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-5", question: "Which change would increase the return on capital employed?", choices: ["Higher profit with the same capital employed", "The owner introducing more capital with the same profit", "Taking a long-term loan and holding the cash in the bank", "Lower profit with the same capital employed"], explanation: "ROCE is profit before interest over capital employed, so more profit on the same funds raises it. Extra capital or a loan held as idle cash increases the bottom of the fraction without adding profit, and lower profit obviously reduces it." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-6", question: "Trade receivables turnover rose from 30 days to 55 days. Which is the most likely explanation?", choices: ["Credit control has weakened or longer credit terms were given", "Customers are paying more quickly", "A larger share of sales is now for cash", "Suppliers are allowing longer credit"], explanation: "More days means credit customers take longer to pay, which points to weaker chasing of debts or more generous terms. Faster payment or more cash sales would reduce the days, and supplier terms affect payables, not receivables." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-7", question: "What is the main risk of allowing trade payables turnover to become very long?", choices: ["Suppliers may refuse further credit or stop supplying", "Trade receivables will increase", "The rate of inventory turnover will rise", "Gross margin will automatically fall"], explanation: "Paying late damages the relationship with suppliers, who may demand cash on delivery or withdraw supply. It does not change receivables or inventory turnover, and the margin falls only if cash discounts are lost, which is not automatic." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-8", question: "A business has a current ratio of 4.00:1. What might this indicate?", choices: ["Too many resources are tied up in inventory, receivables or idle cash", "The business cannot pay its short-term debts", "The business is highly profitable", "The bank overdraft is very large"], explanation: "A very high current ratio means current assets are far larger than needed to cover short-term debts, so money is sitting idle. It does not show profitability, and a large overdraft would make the ratio lower, not higher." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-9", question: "Which pair of ratios measures liquidity?", choices: ["Current ratio and liquid (acid test) ratio", "Gross margin and profit margin", "Return on capital employed and profit margin", "Rate of inventory turnover and gross margin"], explanation: "Liquidity ratios compare current assets with current liabilities. The other pairs are profitability measures or mix a profitability ratio with an efficiency ratio." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-10", question: "A profitable business has rising revenue, rising trade receivables days, falling inventory turnover and a falling liquid ratio. What is this situation called?", choices: ["Overtrading", "Window dressing", "Going concern", "Prudence"], explanation: "Overtrading is growing sales faster than the working capital can support, so cash runs short although profit is made. Window dressing is presenting figures to look better than they are, and going concern and prudence are accounting principles." },
  ],
